WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.898
Later that day, I stopped at the captain’s door with a cool drink.

2
00:00:04.899 --> 00:00:08.298
He seemed weak and excited at the same time.

3
00:00:08.299 --> 00:00:15.595
"Jim," he said, "I’ve been good to you. But now, I’m low and alone.

4
00:00:15.596 --> 00:00:19.066
Bring me some rum, won’t you, matey?"

5
00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:22.371
"The doctor?—," I began.

6
00:00:22.372 --> 00:00:28.980
He cursed the doctor. "Doctors are all swabs, only fit to clean a ship," he said.

7
00:00:28.981 --> 00:00:41.109
"What does the doctor know? I’ve been in places hot as a furnace, with mates falling sick with yellow fever, and I lived on rum, I tell you.

8
00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:50.029
If I can’t have some rum now, I’ll die—and my death will be your fault, Jim." He

9
00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:53.862
cursed some more and then began to plead. "Look

10
00:00:53.863 --> 00:01:00.314
how my fingers are shaking. I haven’t had a drop to drink today. The

11
00:01:00.315 --> 00:01:04.254
doctor himself said one glass wouldn’t hurt me. I’ll

12
00:01:04.255 --> 00:01:07.619
give you a gold coin for a drink, Jim!" He

13
00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:12.886
was growing far too excited and loud, and my father needed peace and quiet. And

14
00:01:12.887 --> 00:01:17.389
the doctor had said one drink wouldn’t hurt. Finally

15
00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:21.989
I said, "I don’t want your money, except what you owe my father. I’ll

16
00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:24.871
get you one glass, and no more." When

17
00:01:24.872 --> 00:01:28.663
I brought it, he seized it greedily and drank. "Aye,"

18
00:01:28.664 --> 00:01:35.389
he said. "That’s better. How long must I lie here?" "A

19
00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:39.182
week, at least," I replied. "Thunder!"

20
00:01:39.183 --> 00:01:43.380
the captain cried loudly. "I can’t do that! They’ll

21
00:01:43.381 --> 00:01:49.449
find out where I am and give me the black spot. I must get away." He

22
00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:56.526
rose from the pillow with great difficulty, holding on to my shoulder with a grip that almost made me cry out. Then

23
00:01:56.527 --> 00:02:02.384
he paused, sat up in bed, and said, "Help me up." But

24
00:02:02.385 --> 00:02:07.354
before I could, he fell back on his pillow. "You

25
00:02:07.355 --> 00:02:11.352
saw that seafaring man today?" "Black

26
00:02:11.353 --> 00:02:13.820
Dog?" I asked. "Yes,

27
00:02:13.821 --> 00:02:20.624
Black Dog. He’s bad, but there are worse men than him from old Flint’s crew. They’re

28
00:02:20.625 --> 00:02:29.456
after my sea chest, boy, and if I can’t get away. . ." The old man coughed. "Captain?"

29
00:02:29.457 --> 00:02:32.297
I said. "You

30
00:02:32.298 --> 00:02:42.244
get on a horse and ride to that doctor and tell him to have them all arrested, all of them, all that’s left of old Flint’s crew!" The

31
00:02:42.245 --> 00:02:46.062
captain closed his eyes, but kept speaking. "I

32
00:02:46.063 --> 00:02:52.419
was Flint’s first mate, and I am the only one who knows the secret place. Flint

33
00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.625
told me when he was dying. But

34
00:02:55.626 --> 00:03:10.649
don’t get the doctor unless Flint’s old crew puts the black spot on me, or if you see Black Dog again, or a seafaring man with one leg—him above all." "But

35
00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:14.583
what’s the black spot?" I asked. "It’s

36
00:03:14.584 --> 00:03:21.850
a judgment, Jim—an evil summons to my own death." I

37
00:03:21.851 --> 00:03:27.804
gave him some medicine, and he fell into a deep sleep. As

38
00:03:27.805 --> 00:03:32.047
it happened, my father died quite suddenly that evening. Our

39
00:03:32.048 --> 00:03:41.964
grief, the visits from the neighbors, the funeral arrangements, and the work at the inn kept me too busy to worry about the captain for a few days. He

40
00:03:41.965 --> 00:03:48.224
came downstairs to eat his meals as usual, but he drank more rum than he had before. He

41
00:03:48.225 --> 00:03:54.617
seemed shut up in his own thoughts, never talking to me or any of the other guests. Then

42
00:03:54.618 --> 00:04:02.851
one frosty afternoon, as I stood at the door, full of sad thoughts, I noticed a blind man outside. He

43
00:04:02.852 --> 00:04:08.191
tapped his way down the road, using a long stick for a cane. He

44
00:04:08.192 --> 00:04:16.956
wore a green cloth over his eyes and a huge, tattered sea cloak with a hood that made him appear deformed. I

45
00:04:16.957 --> 00:04:20.398
never saw a more dreadful-looking figure. He

46
00:04:20.399 --> 00:04:28.635
stopped, and addressing the air, said, "Will any kind friend inform a poor blind man where he is?" "You

47
00:04:28.636 --> 00:04:33.370
are at the Admiral Benbow Inn," I said. "I

48
00:04:33.371 --> 00:04:35.888
hear a young voice," he said. "Will

49
00:04:35.889 --> 00:04:41.186
you give me your hand, my kind young friend, and lead me in?" I

50
00:04:41.187 --> 00:04:47.139
held out my hand, and the horrible, soft-spoken, eyeless creature gripped me so hard it hurt. I

51
00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:51.302
struggled, but the blind man pulled me closer. "Now,

52
00:04:51.303 --> 00:04:56.225
boy," he said, "take me to the captain or I’ll break your arm." He

53
00:04:56.226 --> 00:05:00.431
gave my arm a wrench that made me cry out. "Sir,"

54
00:05:00.432 --> 00:05:03.975
I said, "the captain is ill." "March,"

55
00:05:03.976 --> 00:05:06.715
he commanded. The

56
00:05:06.716 --> 00:05:13.205
blind man held me tight, and I never heard a voice more cruel, cold, and ugly. "Lead

57
00:05:13.206 --> 00:05:18.674
me to him and say, ‘A friend is here to see you, Bill,’ or else I’ll do this ."

58
00:05:18.675 --> 00:05:22.901
And he pinched me so hard I thought I’d faint.

59
00:05:22.902 --> 00:05:27.928
I walked over to the captain’s table and said, "Your friend is here, Bill!"

60
00:05:27.929 --> 00:05:35.590
The poor captain raised his head from his arms on the table; the drunkenness went out of his eyes, and he stared at me sober.

61
00:05:35.591 --> 00:05:37.917
He looked mortally sick.

62
00:05:37.918 --> 00:05:40.754
"Time for business," the blind man said.

63
00:05:40.755 --> 00:05:44.963
"Boy, take his wrist and bring it near to me."

64
00:05:44.964 --> 00:05:48.501
I took the captain’s wrist and he didn’t resist.

65
00:05:48.502 --> 00:05:56.184
I brought his hand toward the blind man and then saw something pass from the blind man’s hand into the captain’s palm.

66
00:05:56.185 --> 00:05:59.857
"And now that’s done," the blind man said.

67
00:05:59.858 --> 00:06:04.952
He let go of me, and with incredible nimbleness, skipped out of the inn.

68
00:06:04.953 --> 00:06:10.911
He went out to the road, his stick tap-tap-tapping into the distance.

69
00:06:10.912 --> 00:06:13.400
The captain looked sharply into his palm.

70
00:06:13.401 --> 00:06:17.715
"Ten o’clock!" he cried. "Six hours."

71
00:06:17.716 --> 00:06:25.237
He sprang to his feet but then staggered backward, putting his hand to his throat.

72
00:06:25.238 --> 00:06:30.510
Swaying for a moment, he then fell with a thud to the floor.

73
00:06:30.511 --> 00:06:35.210
I ran to him at once. "Mother!" I cried. "Help us! Mother!"

74
00:06:35.211 --> 00:06:41.594
But it was too late. The captain was dead.

75
00:06:41.595 --> 00:06:52.333
I burst into tears. It was the second death I’d known, and the sorrow of the first was still fresh in my heart.

